Applied by tools/build-toolchain.sh when the binutils pin is on branch
amiga-2.46.  The pin is still amiga-2.39.0, which selects
tools/patches/binutils/ instead; this set is what a pin that moves gets.

Against bebbo's binutils-gdb, branch amiga-2.46, commit
a50544a917284847c99e97d41c69ece9d5cb2fef (https://franke.ms/git/bebbo/binutils-gdb).

WHAT IT FIXES

-DAMINETXDUO_LTO=ON does not link on an unpatched 2.46: every symbol an LTO
static library defines comes out undefined.

2.46 already carries LTO support for the HUNK target that 2.39 does not have,
so the two diffs in tools/patches/binutils/ are not ported -- their five
repairs are all present here in another form.  gas writes .gnu.lto* as debug
hunks with contents (1c3ca820, bb49f8ee), amiga_write_section_contents writes
the { "LTO1", length } header, amiga_slurp_symbol_table reads .gnu.lto_.symtab,
ld/plugin.c keeps its own copy of the plugin's symbols instead of round-tripping
the IR dummy BFD through the HUNK writer (amiga_lto_save_symbols), and upstream
generic_link_check_archive_element reads a claimed member through
abfd->plugin_dummy_bfd.  What is left is the archive symbol index, which none of
that reaches.

Three causes, none of them in the Amiga back end:

  1. binutils/ar.c.  Upstream dropped `target = plugin_target' from
     open_inarch() and derives the archive target from the first member
     instead, so on this target `ar' now writes a native Amiga library: a
     concatenation of HUNK_UNITs with NO symbol index at all.  What
     `nm --print-armap' prints for one is synthesised at read time from each
     member's HUNK_EXT, and a slim LTO member's HUNK_EXT holds nothing but the
     __gnu_lto_slim marker.  ld therefore cannot resolve one single symbol such
     a library defines.  2.39 wrote `!<arch>' for every archive because its ar
     still forced the plugin target, and its index is computed through the
     plugin, from the IR.

  2. bfd/plugin.c.  Restoring that in ar.c alone gets `ar: invalid operation':
     upstream also removed archive support from plugin_vec.  Put back the four
     entries 2.39 had.

  3. bfd/format.c.  With the archive on the plugin target its members inherit
     that target with target_defaulted false, and 2.46 turned "specified target
     did not match" from fall-through into an outright error.  Every member the
     plugin does not claim -- every .S object -- is then dropped from the index.
     2.39 fell through to the full target search.  Scoped here to the plugin
     target, which is a container and not an object format.

  4. bfd/linker.c.  bebbo's marker handling in
     _bfd_generic_link_add_archive_symbols offers the FIRST slim member of an
     archive and silently skips every later one: it looks __gnu_lto_slim up as
     if it were a symbol, and the entry it creates is bfd_link_hash_new, which
     the type test below marks included and passes over.  Fixed with 1 and 2 in
     place, a slim archive would still contribute exactly one member.

MEASURED, 2026-08-27, a 2.46 toolchain built with this and
objcopy-keep-hunk-relocs.diff applied over the pinned GCC 16.2.0b:

  archive index, one slim LTO object and one .S object

    2.39                  _foo in a.o / _bar in a.o / _asmfn in s.o
    2.46 unpatched        ___gnu_lto_slim in a.o          (s.o absent)
    2.46 with this        _foo in a.o / _bar in a.o / _asmfn in s.o

  the whole tree, -DAMINETXDUO_LTO=ON, built by that toolchain

    build=ok
    hunk_relocs=clean images=121                                    rc=0
    rt_helpers=clean images=41                                      rc=0

  the four shipped images, byte-compared against the same tree built with
  -DAMINETXDUO_LTO=ON on the pinned 2.39 toolchain

    bsdsocket.library    388496 bytes   identical
    tls.library          195708 bytes   identical
    anxnet.device         36108 bytes   identical
    usergroup.library      7840 bytes   identical

The pin did not move on the strength of that: byte-identical output is no
reason to republish a prebuilt toolchain that tools/fetch-toolchain.sh pins by
sha256 on two platforms.

